[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Network, Battery, and Rendering Performance Profiling in Android"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building high-performance Android applications requires a deep understanding of resource utilization. Poor network calls, excessive battery drainage, and dropped UI frames lead to uninstalls and bad user reviews. This tutorial covers how to profile and optimize these three critical pillars using modern Android Studio tools and Kotlin."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Network Profiling"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Network Profiler in Android Studio displays real-time network activity, showing data sent and received, along with the current connection state. To optimize network performance, you should inspect payload sizes, eliminate redundant requests, and use efficient caching strategies with libraries like OkHttp."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"val client = OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .cache(Cache(context.cacheDir, 10 * 1024 * 1024))
    .addInterceptor(HttpLoggingInterceptor().apply {
        level = HttpLoggingInterceptor.Level.BODY
    })
    .build()"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Battery and Power Profiling"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Battery drain is often caused by wake locks, frequent GPS polling, and unoptimized background jobs. The Energy Profiler tracks CPU, Network, and Location usage and correlates them with power consumption events. Use WorkManager for deferrable background work to allow the CPU to enter deep sleep states."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"val constraints = Constraints.Builder()
    .setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.UNMETERED)
    .setRequiresCharging(true)
    .build()

val syncWorkRequest = OneTimeWorkRequestBuilder<SyncWorker>()
    .setConstraints(constraints)
    .build()

WorkManager.getInstance(context).enqueue(syncWorkRequest)"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Rendering Performance Profiling"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Rendering issues manifest as stuttering or frozen UI. Android targets a 60fps or 120fps rendering rate, meaning each frame must be drawn in under 16.6ms (for 60fps). Use the Layout Inspector to detect deep view hierarchies and GPU Profiling / Perfetto traces to find overloaded onDraw() methods or recomposition loops in Jetpack Compose."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid nested LinearLayouts with heavy weight attributes which cause double-taxation measurement passes."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use ConstraintLayout or Jetpack Compose to keep the view tree flat."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Utilize remember and derivedStateOf in Compose to minimize unnecessary recompositions."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@Composable
"OptimizedList"(items: List<String>) {
    LazyColumn {
        items(items, key = { it }) { item ->
            Text(text = item)
        }
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Profiling should be an ongoing part of your development lifecycle. Regularly check the Network, Energy, and CPU/GPU profilers inside Android Studio to catch regressions early and ensure a buttery-smooth user experience."}]
